      <p>English “must not” and “don’t have to” are easy to swap. German marks them with different verbs: <span class="de">du darfst nicht</span> = prohibition; <span class="de">du musst nicht</span> = no obligation. English “I had to work” is a past of must; German uses <span class="de">ich musste arbeiten</span>, not a Perfekt of müssen in careful A2 writing. English “can” covers ability and permission; German splits <span class="de">können</span> (ability) and <span class="de">dürfen</span> (permission). English “should” is soft advice; German <span class="de">sollen</span> in announcements is often a hard instruction: <span class="de">Sie sollen im Wartezimmer Platz nehmen.</span></p>
